ow leads to weight gain. Fast food's high calorie load hits a body that burns far fewer calories than its ancestors did. Finally, **the global scale and marketing**. Fast food chains spend billions advertising to children and families, creating lifelong habits. They strategically locate in low-income neighborhoods and near schools. No historical calorie-dense food has ever had that kind of systematic, worldwide push. So the reason I'm confident isn't that I think fast food is uniquely evil — it's that the *combination* of its engineered addictiveness, massive portions, constant availability, and the modern sedentary lifestyle creates a perfect storm that past generations never faced. That's why we see obesity rates skyrocket precisely as fast food becomes ubiquitous, not when people simply eat the occasional rich meal.
